NAME
    prefs - graphical system preferences

SYNOPSIS
    prefs

DESCRIPTION
    A preferences window split into a pane selector (left)
    and a content area (right):

      About     OS identification (utsname, /proc/version)
      Memory    live totals from /proc/meminfo and /proc/uptime
      Mounts    mounted filesystems from /proc/mounts
      CPU       /proc/cpuinfo snippet
      Wallpaper the pictures in /etc/vnu/pics and the shipped one

KEYS
    j/k switch pane, Esc closes. Click a pane to select it.

    In the Wallpaper pane the Up/Down arrows move the cursor over the
    candidates (clicking one also) and Enter applies the selected one:
    the kernel decodes a picture, or installs the flat colour the
    shipped entry is, and the desktop redraws at once. Something it
    refuses leaves the desktop as it was and says why on the status
    line. See man wallpaper for what may be used, and note that the
    choice is forgotten at reboot.

    F12 belongs to the desktop rather than to this window, so it works
    with every pane and with no window at all: it steps the display
    through 640x480, 800x600, 1024x768 and 1280x1024 while the desktop
    keeps running, moving windows back inside the new screen. The mode
    is recorded in /etc/vnu/config/gfx.conf, so the next gui and the
    next boot come up in it, and /proc/gfx reports the one in use.
    Like everything else in the RAM filesystem, that file is gone at
    reboot, so a booted ISO starts over from the built-in 1024x768.
    A GUI app.
